Energy released in the fission of one nucleus
$\begin{aligned}
&amp; =200 \mathrm{MeV} \\
&amp; =200 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-13} \mathrm{~J} \\
&amp; =3.2 \times 10^{-11} \mathrm{~J}
\end{aligned}$
Energy to be released
$=1000 \mathrm{~J}3.2 \times 10^{-11} \mathrm{~J}$ energy is released due to number of nucleus fissioned $=1\therefore 1000 \mathrm{~J}$ energy is released due to number of nucleus fissioned
$\begin{aligned}
&amp; =\frac{1 \times 1000}{3.2 \times 10^{-11}} \\
&amp; =3.125 \times 10^{13} \text { nucleus }
\end{aligned}$

